  "query_preview": "..\n\nQuasi-zonal upper level flow over portions of the Pacific Northwest \nand southern Canada will bring low amplitude ridging to the Central \nGreat Lakes through at least the middle portions of the weekend \nperiod. Calm winds and time of year considerations suggests some fog \npotential at daybreak Friday morning. Surface condensation pressure \ndeficits point to some likelihood particularly in the Thumb down to \nthe I69 corridor. MOS guidance also shows some signal and decided to \nadd patchy fog to the gridded database. Temperatures will be in the \nupper 70s to around 80 degrees each of the next 3 days which is at \nor slightly above normal.\n\nModels suggest that by late Saturday there is a slight chance for \nsome showers across southern portions of the forecast area during \npeak heating. From a macro perspective, weak deformation forcing can \narise out of latent heating churn that typically occurs inside of an \nupper level ridge. In this case there is a ribbon of potential \nvorticity that is resolved near the Ohio River that begins to \nmigrate northward. Big limitation will be the residual subsidence \nthat resides in the 2.0 to 7.0 kft agl layer that will serve as a \ngood capping inversion.   \n\nTroughing then enters and expands through Lower Michigan Sunday \npushing a cold front through the region. With post frontal mixing \ntemperatures are not expected to cool that much for Sunday but \ndewpoints will be some 8 to 10 degrees lower. Chilly conditions are \nexpected Sunday night with some 40s possible in Midland county by \nMonday morning. Another round of ridging is anticipated for Monday \nand Tuesday.",
